ost-quantum cryptography means  cryptography for classical computers or  classical machines interacting with each  other  where security holds against quantum  attacks that's what post quantum means  and so the minimum requirement for  post-quantum cryptography  is that you base your cryptosystems on  underlying hard problems that resist  quantum attacks unfortunately we have uh  candidate heart problems so various  problems on lattices isogenys and so on  so forth  uh but a common misconception is that  this is sort of the only thing you need  to look at right that you can take some  classically secure cryptosystem and then  replace your quantumly broken assumption  for one that is a quantumly secure right  like you might think that this this  fixes the problem but this is not  actually the only consideration this is  a common misconception that you just  need to fix the assumption  and what i'm getting at here the key  point is that there's another half of  the equation that you have to pay  attention to  so in a security proof you need both a  post quantum hardness assumption and a  security reduction that explicitly  handles quantum computers  so what i mean by that is if you already  proved security in the classical setting  you probably wrote down a classical  reduction  and that reduction showed that any  classical attack on the protocol leads  to a classical attack on the assumption  but now you're trying to claim security  against quantum attacks and so you need  to make sure your reduction actually  says the bottom thing which is that any  quantum attack on the protocol implies a  quantum attack on the assumption  so this is what i call a quantum  compatible reduction and it's essential  that you actually have  such a reduction to claim any post  quantum security  so many classical reductions are in fact  quantum compatible if you stare at it a  little bit you'll realize that a lot of  reductions do handle quantum computers  but phantograph in his 98 phd thesis  observed that there is a large class of  reductions in cryptography  that are not quantum compatible  and the reductions that do something  called rewinding  so if you haven't seen it before i don't  really have time to get into the details  but i will show you the underlying  mechanic of what it means to rewind  so  what does it mean to rewind well say you  have some interactive protocol and  you're trying to prove security against  say some malicious approver  so what this reduction might do is have  a regular conversation with the prover  as if it's the other party in the  interaction  write down the transcript of the  interaction  and then it's going to reset the prover  to its initial to an earlier state in  the interaction  and from that reset state it's going to  have another interaction with this  prover and then write down that  interaction  so the goal of what you do with  rewinding in the security reduction is  you use this property to extract many  transcripts from the adversary  and then together you might use these  transcripts to break some heart problem  so what goes wrong if this malicious  proverb is potentially a quantum  computer well this very first step right  the first time you interact with this  adversary the way that it computes its  classical answers might be through  measurement  and if it's doing measurements then it  might actually destroy its internal  state  and then it's unclear how you can rewind  back to an earlier point in the protocol  and get more transcripts out of this  adversary so this style of arguing  security just doesn't work it works  totally fine on classical computers  because you can just reset in an earlier  state but it doesn't work in the quantum  setting  okay so uh this has been a major problem  for over two decades now and uh there  were some prior works that were uh able  to show some limited positive results  for quantum rewinding  in particular this work of unru from  2012 showed that there is some amount of  rewinding you can do you can get a  constant number of accepting transcripts  from a quantum adversary  and this is enough for some settings but  it's insufficient for some really  important uh  results in cryptography and the one that  i'm focusing on today is this uh  primitive of succinct arguments for np  okay so uh before i go on what is a  succinct argument  it's basically one of the most  uh basic settings you have in computer  science approver is trying to convince a  verifier that something is true  in particular uh they've agreed on some  np instance in the threesat instance  and the prover has a witness and the  approver just wants to convince the  verifier that that it's a yes instance  and the sustained part of sync arguments  means that we want the total  communication to be much shorter than  actually sending the whole witness over  so we want a protocol that allows the  prover to do much better than sending  the witness to the verifier we want much  less communication and in particular we  want something like poly logarithmic in  the witness size  it's an exponential improvement over to  sending the witness  and uh the argument part just means that  the soundness condition the soundness is  only supposed to hold against efficient  cheating proverbs strategies  so it's not unconditional soundness is  you only have soundness the verifier  won't be fooled as long as the  malicious prover is efficient  so being fooled means that it acts as a  no instance that the verifier would end  up accepting  okay so a seminal result of killian from  1992 is that succinct arguments for np  exist for any np language assuming just  collision resistant hash functions which  are a relatively mild cryptographic  assumption  and if it's something you've never heard  about i just want to take a moment and  pause here like i think this is a really  like  beautiful statement but uh you can use  cryptography to solve this problem of  efficient verification that on its